{"content_hash": "cd2d23050caf5d99", "version_id": 583, "article_id": 59, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Iran war puts Healey's Budget under pressure, think tank warns", "title_zh": "智库警告：伊朗战争让希利的预算计划压力山大", "has_translation": true, "topic": "business", "source": "bbc_business", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm78pwdp3n5qo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1693139062697-f5f8663217fa?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8MXx8aXJhbiUyMHdhciUyMHRocmVhdGVuc3xlbnwwfDB8fHwxNzkxNzY1NjgyfDA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by sina drakhshani on Unsplash", "author": "sina drakhshani", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@sina_drakhshani?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/flag-of-iran-waving-9S96Rqy9Q1U?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(107, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(145, 46%, 48%)", "label": "BUSINESS", "seed": "56335abab054"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-12", "word_count": 203, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "A new war in Iran has pushed up prices around the world. Now a top think tank says this could make life very hard for the UK's new chancellor, John Healey, as he writes his first Budget.", "lead_zh": "伊朗战争推高了全球物价。一家顶级智库现在表示，这可能会让英国新财相约翰·希利在制定首份预算时非常头疼。", "preview": "The US-Israel war against Iran has caused energy prices to rise. Because of this, the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) warns that inflatio", "preview_zh": "美国-以色列对伊朗的战争导致能源价格上涨。因此，财政研究所（IFS）警告说，通胀可能高于预期。这意味着政府用于公共服务的资金可能会减少。希利现在必须在削减开支、增税或两者兼施之间做出选择。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 571, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/59/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 83.188, "size_bytes": 502992, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 239, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@2bf0132/content/data/versions/583.json", "paragraphs": ["The US-Israel war against Iran has caused energy prices to rise. Because of this, the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) warns that inflation could be higher than expected. This means the government may have less money to spend on public services. Healey must now choose between cutting spending, raising taxes, or doing both.", "The IFS also said the government should be honest about its spending plans. It should not promise future cuts that it does not really plan to make. A Treasury spokesperson declined to comment on what they called 'rumour and speculation'. However, Healey said the conflict in the Middle East and Europe is hurting countries everywhere.", "Healey said his Budget will focus on 'fiscal discipline' to help the economy. But the government is under pressure to show financial markets it can control its borrowing. The IFS thinks higher inflation will add about £10bn to borrowing costs by 2029-30. To keep budgets in line with inflation, the government may need to find an extra £25bn.", "The IFS also warned that a 'steady-as-she-goes' Budget is not possible. If Healey keeps the old tax and spending plans, inflation will blow departments' budgets off course. The Chancellor will give his Budget speech on 28 October."], "paragraphs_zh": ["美国-以色列对伊朗的战争导致能源价格上涨。因此，财政研究所（IFS）警告说，通胀可能高于预期。这意味着政府用于公共服务的资金可能会减少。希利现在必须在削减开支、增税或两者兼施之间做出选择。", "IFS还表示，政府应该对其支出计划保持诚实。它不应承诺未来并不打算真正实施的削减。财政部发言人拒绝就他们所称的“谣言和猜测”置评。然而，希利表示，中东和欧洲的冲突正在伤害世界各地的国家。", "希利说，他的预算将侧重于“财政纪律”以帮助经济。但政府面临压力，需要向金融市场表明它能够控制借贷。IFS认为，更高的通胀将在2029-30年前使借贷成本增加约100亿英镑。为了使预算与通胀保持一致，政府可能需要额外筹集250亿英镑。", "IFS还警告说，“稳扎稳打”的预算是不可能的。如果希利保留旧的税收和支出计划，通胀将使各部门的预算偏离轨道。财相将于10月28日发表预算演讲。"], "body": "The US-Israel war against Iran has caused energy prices to rise. Because of this, the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) warns that inflation could be higher than expected. This means the government may have less money to spend on public services. Healey must now choose between cutting spending, raising taxes, or doing both.\n\nThe IFS also said the government should be honest about its spending plans. It should not promise future cuts that it does not really plan to make. A Treasury spokesperson declined to comment on what they called 'rumour and speculation'. However, Healey said the conflict in the Middle East and Europe is hurting countries everywhere.\n\nHealey said his Budget will focus on 'fiscal discipline' to help the economy. But the government is under pressure to show financial markets it can control its borrowing. The IFS thinks higher inflation will add about £10bn to borrowing costs by 2029-30. To keep budgets in line with inflation, the government may need to find an extra £25bn.\n\nThe IFS also warned that a 'steady-as-she-goes' Budget is not possible. If Healey keeps the old tax and spending plans, inflation will blow departments' budgets off course. The Chancellor will give his Budget speech on 28 October.", "body_zh": "美国-以色列对伊朗的战争导致能源价格上涨。因此，财政研究所（IFS）警告说，通胀可能高于预期。这意味着政府用于公共服务的资金可能会减少。希利现在必须在削减开支、增税或两者兼施之间做出选择。\n\nIFS还表示，政府应该对其支出计划保持诚实。它不应承诺未来并不打算真正实施的削减。财政部发言人拒绝就他们所称的“谣言和猜测”置评。然而，希利表示，中东和欧洲的冲突正在伤害世界各地的国家。\n\n希利说，他的预算将侧重于“财政纪律”以帮助经济。但政府面临压力，需要向金融市场表明它能够控制借贷。IFS认为，更高的通胀将在2029-30年前使借贷成本增加约100亿英镑。为了使预算与通胀保持一致，政府可能需要额外筹集250亿英镑。\n\nIFS还警告说，“稳扎稳打”的预算是不可能的。如果希利保留旧的税收和支出计划，通胀将使各部门的预算偏离轨道。财相将于10月28日发表预算演讲。", "text": "The US-Israel war against Iran has caused energy prices to rise. Because of this, the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) warns that inflation could be higher than expected. This means the government may have less money to spend on public services. Healey must now choose between cutting spending, raising taxes, or doing both. The IFS also said the government should be honest about its spending plans. It should not promise future cuts that it does not really plan to make. A Treasury spokesperson declined to comment on what they called 'rumour and speculation'. However, Healey said the conflict in the Middle East and Europe is hurting countries everywhere. Healey said his Budget will focus on 'fiscal discipline' to help the economy. But the government is under pressure to show financial markets it can control its borrowing. The IFS thinks higher inflation will add about £10bn to borrowing costs by 2029-30. To keep budgets in line with inflation, the government may need to find an extra £25bn. The IFS also warned that a 'steady-as-she-goes' Budget is not possible. If Healey keeps the old tax and spending plans, inflation will blow departments' budgets off course. The Chancellor will give his Budget speech on 28 October.", "vocab": [{"word": "inflation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "通货膨胀", "note": "物价上涨，货币购买力下降", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "think tank", "pos": "noun", "zh": "智库", "note": "提供政策建议的研究机构", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "chancellor", "pos": "noun", "zh": "财政大臣", "note": "英国负责财政的大臣", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "budget", "pos": "noun", "zh": "预算", "note": "政府或机构的收支计划", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "borrowing", "pos": "noun", "zh": "借贷", "note": "政府借入的资金", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "fiscal discipline", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "财政纪律", "note": "控制政府支出和债务的规则", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "headroom", "pos": "noun", "zh": "缓冲空间", "note": "在财政规则下可用的额外资金", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "credible", "pos": "adj", "zh": "可信的", "note": "被认为是真实或可靠的", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "pencilling in", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "暂定", "note": "初步计划，可能改变", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "steady-as-she-goes", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "稳扎稳打", "note": "保持现状，不改变方向", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Iran war threatens Healey's Budget plans, think tank warns", "summary_original": "The impact of the Iran war poses significant risks for the government’s spending plans, the Institute for Fiscal Studies says.", "body_original": "Iran war threatens Healey's Budget plans, think tank warns\nBy\nDharshini David\n,\nDeputy economics editor\nand\nTom Espiner\n,\nBusiness reporter\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nThe price spike caused by the US-Israel war in Iran poses a major challenge for Chancellor John Healey as he puts together his first Budget, an influential think tank has warned.\nHigher-than-expected inflation could eat into what the government hoped to spend on public services, meaning Healey will have to choose between spending cuts, tax rises, or a mixture of both, the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) said.\nIt urged the government to ensure its spending plans are credible, as opposed to proposing unrealistic future cuts to make the Budget add up.\nThe Treasury declined to comment on \"rumour and speculation\".\nHowever, speaking ahead of the annual International Monetary Fund meeting this week, Healey said: \"The consequences of conflict in the Middle East and Europe are hitting countries worldwide, leading to higher prices at the pumps and more expensive mortgages at home.\n\"But Britain and our allies can face up to these challenges together.\"\nHe said his Budget \"will build on this approach with fiscal discipline helping to deliver economic resilience\" and give people \"breathing space\".\nHowever, the government is under growing pressure to show financial markets that it has a tight grip on finances as the cost of borrowing rises.\nThe IFS cautioned Healey about making any spending announcements in the 28 October Budget speech he has no intention of honouring.\n\"A 'steady-as-she-goes' Budget is not an option available to the chancellor,\" said IFS director Helen Miller said.\n\"Choosing to stay the course with the inherited tax and spending plans would mean allowing inflation to blow departments' spending plans off course.\n\"Simply pencilling in implicit cuts that he has no intention of delivering would make the Budget a work of fiction,\" she added.\nThe US-Israel war against Iran and Russia's invasion of Ukraine have together helped drive up energy prices around the world.\nThese higher costs have been passed to customers via higher prices on shelves and at the pumps.\nThe wars have also contributed to a rise in the cost of UK government debt interest payments, adding to the pressure on Healey.\nAlthough higher inflation will boost tax revenues, it will also eat into the value of departments' cash budgets, the IFS said.\n'Breathing space'\nLike other analysts, the IFS thinks recent turbulence on bond markets and higher inflation will add around £10bn to forecasts for the government's borrowing costs in 2029-30.\nHowever, it still expects the government to meet its self-imposed fiscal rules - that it will only borrow for investment projects by the financial year 2029-30 and also be reducing debt as a percentage of GDP - not least as inflation pushes up tax revenues.\nBut it expects those rules to be met by a smaller buffer - known as \"headroom\" - of around £20bn. That is less than the £24bn buffer predicted at the time of the Spring Statement by the official forecaster, the Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR).\nRecent indications have been that the markets would be comfortable with that degree of reduced headroom. The IFS also expects any \"breathing space\" help for households to be temporary and limited in scale.\nHowever, the IFS warns that in such a volatile environment, its forecasts are exceptionally uncertain. And the official forecast from the OBR - which the chancellor relies on when making his plans - may come to a very different conclusion.\nThe IFS reckons that to maintain departments' budgets in the face of higher inflation, the government may need to find an extra £25bn in 2029-30.\nThat is before consider considering the ambition to raise defence spending. Raising such an amount would point to significant tax rises.\nThe chancellor does not have to address spending totals in this Budget, and may choose to leave such decisions until future events - for example, next year's Spring Statement or Spending Review - by which time the outlook for the economy could look very different.\nBut the IFS warned that the spending plans inherited from the previous chancellor, Rachel Reeves, would mean some departments' budgets would not keep pace with inflation in a few years time.\nIt said not addressing this would cast doubt on the credibility of the government's plans.\nHowever, previous chancellors have relied on a similar strategy of pencilling in unrealistic future spending plans to ensure that they meet the fiscal rules on the day - only to change the plans further down the line.\nRelated topics\nUK economy\nInstitute for Fiscal Studies\nJohn Healey\nMore on this story\nWhat is the Budget and what could be in it?\nPublished\n3 days ago", "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T00:41:21.254686", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.238, "sm": 100, "em": 237, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "US-Israel", "s": 0.25, "e": 1.05, "sm": 250, "em": 1050, "cs": 4, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "war", "s": 1.062, "e": 1.312, "sm": 1062, "em": 1312, "cs": 14, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "against", "s": 1.325, "e": 1.725, "sm": 1325, "em": 1725, "cs": 18, "ce": 25, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "Iran", "s": 1.738, "e": 2.125, "sm": 1737, "em": 2125, "cs": 26, "ce": 30, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "has", "s": 2.138, "e": 2.312, "sm": 2137, "em": 2312, "cs": 31, "ce": 34, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "caused", "s": 2.325, "e": 2.738, "sm": 2325, "em": 2737, "cs": 35, "ce": 41, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "energy", "s": 2.75, "e": 3.125, "sm": 2750, "em": 3125, "cs": 42, "ce": 48, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "prices", "s": 3.138, "e": 3.575, "sm": 3137, "em": 3575, "cs": 49, "ce": 55, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "to", "s": 3.587, "e": 3.712, "sm": 3587, "em": 3712, "cs": 56, "ce": 58, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "rise", "s": 3.725, "e": 4.275, "sm": 3725, "em": 4275, "cs": 59, "ce": 63, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": ".", "s": 4.275, "e": 5